UNIT toArray
KIND FUNCTIONAL
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG toArray(value: any) -> Array          # default export: module.exports = toArray
DEPENDS-ON none                            # self-contained single file, ZERO runtime deps

# =============================================================================
# RECONSTRUCTION DIRECTIVE
# =============================================================================
# This is the DEFAULT export of lodash.toarray@4.4.0. The re-emitter MUST:
#     export default <fn>
# where <fn> is the reconstructed `toArray`. There is no named export.
# Pure / read-only: NEVER mutates `value`; the only "copy" branch (copyArray)
# allocates a fresh Array. No clock / RNG / fs / net seam -> deterministic.

# =============================================================================
# BEHAVIOR  (exact dispatch order — this is the spine; order is load-bearing)
# =============================================================================
#
# toArray(value):
#
# NODE 1 — FALSY GUARD
#   if (!value) return []
#     `!value` is true for: null, undefined, false, 0, -0, NaN, "" (empty string),
#     0n (BigInt zero). ALL of these short-circuit to a NEW empty array [].
#     NOTE: the empty string "" is caught HERE (it is falsy) and never reaches
#           the string-split path. This is a real edge: toArray("") === [].
#
# NODE 2 — ARRAY-LIKE BRANCH
#   if (isArrayLike(value)):
#       return isString(value) ? stringToArray(value) : copyArray(value)
#
#   isArrayLike(value)  ::=  value != null
#                            && isLength(value.length)
#                            && !isFunction(value)
#     isLength(n) ::= typeof n == 'number' && n > -1 && n % 1 == 0
#                     && n <= 9007199254740991   (MAX_SAFE_INTEGER)
#       => length must be a NON-NEGATIVE INTEGER in [0, 2^53-1]. A length that is
#          non-integer (1.5), negative (-1), > MAX_SAFE_INTEGER (9007199254740992),
#          or non-number is NOT array-like.
#     isFunction(value) ::= getTag in {'[object Function]','[object GeneratorFunction]'}
#          (functions are excluded — but functions are OUT-OF-ENVELOPE here, see SCOPE)
#
#   Values that ARE array-like (and thus take this branch):
#     * primitive strings (typeof 'string'); length = the JS .length (UTF-16 units)
#     * boxed String objects [out-of-envelope, see SCOPE]
#     * real Arrays (incl. SPARSE arrays — holes become undefined via copyArray)
#     * typed arrays: Uint8Array, Int8Array, Uint16Array, Int16Array, Uint32Array,
#       Int32Array, Float32Array, Float64Array, Uint8ClampedArray (all have .length)
#     * arguments objects
#     * Buffer (it is a Uint8Array subclass -> array-like)
#     * plain objects carrying an integer .length: {length:N, 0:.., 1:..}
#
#   isString(value) ::= typeof value == 'string'
#                       || (!isArray(value) && isObjectLike(value)
#                           && getTag(value) == '[object String]')
#     => true for primitive strings and boxed String objects ONLY.
#
#   NODE 2a — STRING  (isString true): return stringToArray(value)  [see STRING ALGO]
#   NODE 2b — NON-STRING ARRAY-LIKE: return copyArray(value)
#       copyArray(source):
#         length = source.length
#         array = Array(length)            # pre-sized
#         for index in 0..length-1: array[index] = source[index]
#         return array
#       => SHALLOW copy of indices 0..length-1, reading source[index] (so
#          {length:3, 0:'a'} -> ['a', undefined, undefined]; SPARSE array holes
#          -> undefined, producing a DENSE result of exactly `length` elements).
#          Keys other than 0..length-1 are IGNORED. Element VALUES are copied by
#          reference (not deep-cloned).
#
# NODE 3 — ITERATOR-SYMBOL BRANCH  (only reached if NOT array-like)
#   if (Symbol.iterator && value[Symbol.iterator]):
#       return iteratorToArray(value[Symbol.iterator]())
#     iteratorToArray drains the iterator: push data.value until data.done.
#     IMPORTANT: array-likes (arrays, typed arrays, strings, arguments) are ALL
#     caught by NODE 2 FIRST, so this branch handles only NON-array-like iterables
#     (e.g. a custom object exposing only Symbol.iterator, or — relevant here —
#     it does NOT fire for Map/Set because... ACTUALLY Map/Set DO have
#     Symbol.iterator; see VERIFIED note below).
#
#   *** VERIFIED ORDERING NOTE (Map/Set vs iterator) ***
#     Map and Set both expose Symbol.iterator. They are NOT array-like (no
#     numeric .length). So they REACH node 3 and `value[Symbol.iterator]` is
#     truthy. HOWEVER the value passed to iteratorToArray is the iterator object,
#     and iterating a Map yields [k,v] pairs and a Set yields values — which is
#     the SAME observable result as mapToArray / setToArray below. In THIS build
#     `iteratorSymbol` resolves via getNative(root,'Symbol') ... Symbol.iterator;
#     for a Map, value[Symbol.iterator] IS present, so NODE 3 fires for Map/Set
#     and returns the iterator-drained array. The observable contract is:
#         Map -> [[k0,v0],[k1,v1],...]   (entry pairs, INSERTION ORDER)
#         Set -> [v0,v1,...]             (values, INSERTION ORDER)
#     This is IDENTICAL to what NODE 4 (mapToArray/setToArray) would produce, so
#     a re-emitter may implement EITHER node-3-iterator OR node-4-getTag dispatch
#     for Map/Set and obtain the same observable result. (Both were checked: the
#     ENTRY-PAIR shape for Map and the VALUE shape for Set, in insertion order,
#     are the contract. Re-setting an existing Map key updates its value in place
#     and KEEPS its original position.)
#
# NODE 4 — GETTAG DISPATCH  (only reached if not falsy, not array-like, no iterator)
#   tag = getTag(value)            # Object.prototype.toString.call(value)
#   func = tag == '[object Map]' ? mapToArray
#        : tag == '[object Set]' ? setToArray
#        : values
#   return func(value)
#
#     mapToArray(map): result = Array(map.size); index=-1;
#         map.forEach((value,key) => result[++index] = [key, value]); return result
#       => array of [key, value] PAIRS in Map insertion order.
#     setToArray(set): result = Array(set.size); index=-1;
#         set.forEach(value => result[++index] = value); return result
#       => array of values in Set insertion order.
#     values(object): object ? baseValues(object, keys(object)) : []
#       baseValues = keys.map(k => object[k])   # the VALUES, in key order
#       keys(object) = isArrayLike(object) ? arrayLikeKeys(object) : baseKeys(object)
#         For a NON-array-like plain object this is baseKeys -> Object.keys(object):
#         => OWN ENUMERABLE string keys in JS spec order:
#              (1) integer-index keys in ASCENDING NUMERIC order, THEN
#              (2) remaining string keys in INSERTION order.
#            (Symbol keys excluded.)  baseKeys also drops 'constructor' on
#            prototype-like objects.
#       => plain object {b:2,a:1, 2:'two', 1:'one', 10:'ten'} yields keys
#          ['1','2','10','b','a'] -> values ['one','two','ten',2,1].
#
#   *** TRUTHY NON-COLLECTION PRIMITIVES (number/boolean/bigint/symbol) ***
#     A truthy number (5, 3.14), boolean true, or non-zero BigInt (5n) is NOT
#     falsy (skip node1), NOT array-like (no integer .length; skip node2), has no
#     [Symbol.iterator] (skip node3), and reaches `values(prim)`. keys(prim) ->
#     baseKeys(prim) -> Object.keys(prim) == [] (primitives have no own enumerable
#     props), so values(prim) == []. RESULT: toArray(5) == toArray(true) ==
#     toArray(5n) == []. VERIFIED.
#
# =============================================================================
# STRING ALGO — stringToArray  (UNICODE-AWARE; must be reconstructed EXACTLY)
# =============================================================================
# stringToArray(string) = hasUnicode(string) ? unicodeToArray(string)
#                                             : asciiToArray(string)
#
#   asciiToArray(string) = string.split('')   # split into UTF-16 code units
#
#   hasUnicode(string) = reHasUnicode.test(string)   where
#     reHasUnicode = RegExp('[' + ZWJ + ASTRAL + COMBO_MARKS + COMBO_SYMBOLS + VAR + ']')
#       ZWJ          = '\\u200d'
#       ASTRAL       = '\\ud800-\\udfff'
#       COMBO_MARKS  = '\\u0300-\\u036f\\ufe20-\\ufe23'
#       COMBO_SYMBOLS= '\\u20d0-\\u20f0'
#       VAR          = '\\ufe0e\\ufe0f'
#     i.e. hasUnicode is TRUE iff the string contains ANY code unit in the set
#       { U+200D, U+D800..U+DFFF (any surrogate, lone or paired),
#         U+0300..U+036F, U+FE20..U+FE23, U+20D0..U+20F0, U+FE0E, U+FE0F }.
#     If FALSE -> plain split('') (each UTF-16 unit becomes one element).
#       (so "abc"->["a","b","c"]; precomposed "café" with U+00E9 -> 4 units;
#        "\t\n"->["\t","\n"].)
#
#   unicodeToArray(string) = string.match(reUnicode) || []
#     Splits the string into UNICODE GRAPHEME-ISH SYMBOLS using reUnicode (a global
#     regex). The exact regex, reconstructed from these named pieces:
#
#       # ranges
#       rsAstralRange      = '\\ud800-\\udfff'
#       rsComboMarksRange  = '\\u0300-\\u036f\\ufe20-\\ufe23'
#       rsComboSymbolsRange= '\\u20d0-\\u20f0'
#       rsVarRange         = '\\ufe0e\\ufe0f'
#       # capture groups
#       rsAstral    = '[' + rsAstralRange + ']'
#       rsCombo     = '[' + rsComboMarksRange + rsComboSymbolsRange + ']'
#       rsFitz      = '\\ud83c[\\udffb-\\udfff]'        # Fitzpatrick skin-tone modifiers
#       rsModifier  = '(?:' + rsCombo + '|' + rsFitz + ')'
#       rsNonAstral = '[^' + rsAstralRange + ']'
#       rsRegional  = '(?:\\ud83c[\\udde6-\\uddff]){2}' # PAIR of regional indicators
#       rsSurrPair  = '[\\ud800-\\udbff][\\udc00-\\udfff]'  # a high+low surrogate pair
#       rsZWJ       = '\\u200d'
#       # composites
#       reOptMod    = rsModifier + '?'
#       rsOptVar    = '[' + rsVarRange + ']?'
#       rsOptJoin   = '(?:' + rsZWJ + '(?:' + [rsNonAstral, rsRegional, rsSurrPair].join('|')
#                     + ')' + rsOptVar + reOptMod + ')*'
#       rsSeq       = rsOptVar + reOptMod + rsOptJoin
#       rsSymbol    = '(?:' + [rsNonAstral + rsCombo + '?', rsCombo, rsRegional,
#                              rsSurrPair, rsAstral].join('|') + ')'
#       # FINAL regex (the 'g' flag is REQUIRED — match() returns all matches):
#       reUnicode   = RegExp(rsFitz + '(?=' + rsFitz + ')|' + rsSymbol + rsSeq, 'g')
#
#     Expanded, reUnicode source string is (verify byte-for-byte):
#       \ud83c[\udffb-\udfff](?=\ud83c[\udffb-\udfff])|
#       (?:[^\ud800-\udfff][̀-ͯ︠-︣⃐-⃰]?|
#          [̀-ͯ︠-︣⃐-⃰]|
#          (?:\ud83c[\udde6-\uddff]){2}|
#          [\ud800-\udbff][\udc00-\udfff]|
#          [\ud800-\udfff])
#       [︎️]?(?:[̀-ͯ︠-︣⃐-⃰]|\ud83c[\udffb-\udfff])?
#       (?:‍(?:[^\ud800-\udfff]|(?:\ud83c[\udde6-\uddff]){2}|[\ud800-\udbff][\udc00-\udfff])
#          [︎️]?(?:[̀-ͯ︠-︣⃐-⃰]|\ud83c[\udffb-\udfff])?)*
#     (the two long sub-patterns above are rsCombo and the rsModifier alternation;
#      they repeat literally — do NOT abbreviate them in the reconstruction.)
#
#     INTUITION of what each match (= one output element) captures:
#       * a base symbol (non-astral char, a lone/paired combining mark, a regional-
#         indicator PAIR, a surrogate PAIR, or an astral half) ...
#       * optionally followed by a variation selector (FE0E/FE0F), ...
#       * optionally followed by one modifier (a combining mark OR a Fitzpatrick), ...
#       * optionally followed by ANY NUMBER of ZWJ-joined continuations (ZWJ + base +
#         optVar + optMod), so ZWJ emoji sequences collapse into ONE element.
#       * the leading `rsFitz(?=rsFitz)` alternative consumes a Fitzpatrick modifier
#         only when ANOTHER Fitzpatrick immediately follows (lookahead) — a corner
#         case so two consecutive bare Fitzpatricks are split, not joined.
#
#     VERIFIED grapheme behaviors (input -> output array):
#       "a\u{1F600}b"            -> ["a","😀","b"]            (astral pair = 1 elem)
#       "café"  (e+U+0301)       -> ["c","a","f","é"]         (e+combining = 1 elem)
#       "👨‍👩‍👧"      -> ["👨‍👩‍👧"]                 (ZWJ family = 1 elem)
#       "🇺🇸"  (1F1FA 1F1F8)      -> ["🇺🇸"]                   (regional PAIR = 1 elem)
#       "👍🏻"  (1F44D 1F3FB)      -> ["👍🏻"]                   (base+Fitz = 1 elem)
#       "1️"                 -> ["1️"]                    (char+VS16 = 1 elem)
#       "a⃝"                 -> ["a⃝"]                    (base+combo symbol)
#       "\uD800"  (lone high)     -> ["\uD800"]               (lone surrogate = 1)
#       "\uDC00"  (lone low)      -> ["\uDC00"]               (lone surrogate = 1)
#       "\u{1F600}́"         -> ["😀́"]                    (astral+combo = 1)
#       "‍a"  (leading ZWJ)  -> ["‍","a"]                 (bare ZWJ then "a")
#       "‍"   (bare ZWJ)     -> ["‍"]                     (single ZWJ = 1 elem)
#       "️"   (bare VS16)    -> ["️"]                      (single VS = 1 elem)
#       "\u{1F1FA}" (single RI)   -> ["🇺"]                     (odd RI -> surr pair)
#       "🇺🇸🇬"  (three RIs)        -> ["🇺🇸","🇬"]               (pair + leftover)
#       "\u{1F3FB}\u{1F3FC}"      -> ["🏻","🏼"]                (two bare Fitz split)
#
# =============================================================================
# ORACLE value -> oracle.json
# =============================================================================
# Pure deterministic function. The harness calls toArray(args[0]) on the real
# package and on the re-emitted unit; expected = the returned Array. No seam,
# no trace, no carried data.
#
# RESULT-SHAPE NOTE for the codec: outputs may contain `undefined` elements
# (from copyArray over {length:N} gaps / sparse arrays). The grader compares
# arrays element-wise; an `undefined` element and the array's `.length` are both
# part of the contract. (JSON.stringify renders such holes as null but the
# in-memory result is a DENSE array of `.length` slots, indices missing a source
# value holding `undefined`.) The generator AVOIDS emitting array-likes whose
# copyArray output would contain `undefined` ONLY where that undefined would be
# lost by the codec; see generator notes — it uses {length,0,1,...} fully filled
# for the array-like-object cases, plus one documented partial-fill case.

# =============================================================================
# SCOPE
# =============================================================================
# IN-CONTRACT LOGIC (reproduce exactly — every branch above):
#   * the 4-node dispatch (falsy / array-like[string|copy] / iterator / getTag[map|set|values])
#   * isArrayLike + isLength threshold ([0, 2^53-1] non-neg integer length)
#   * copyArray shallow index-copy semantics (dense result of exactly .length)
#   * the FULL unicode stringToArray algorithm (hasUnicode test + reUnicode split)
#     including astral pairs, combining marks (U+0300-036F, U+FE20-FE23, U+20D0-20F0),
#     variation selectors (U+FE0E/FE0F), Fitzpatrick (U+1F3FB-1F3FF), ZWJ sequences
#     (U+200D), regional-indicator pairs, lone surrogates
#   * Map -> [[k,v],...] insertion order ; Set -> [v,...] insertion order
#   * plain object -> values() in Object.keys order (ints ascending, then strings
#     in insertion order)
#   * empty-return cases: null, undefined, false, 0, -0, NaN, "", 0n, AND truthy
#     non-collection primitives (numbers, booleans, non-zero bigints) -> []
#
# CARRIED DATA: none. All literals are small inline regex fragments documented
#   verbatim above; no large table to attest. No carried.json is written.
#
# OUT-OF-CONTRACT / OUT-OF-ENVELOPE (documented; NOT emitted by genInputs because
#   they do not survive the JSON+encode codec round-trip, OR are environment/identity
#   dependent — a blind re-emitter still reconstructs their behavior from the spec):
#   * functions / generator functions -> isFunction true -> not array-like; they
#     have no integer .length and no own enumerable values, so values(fn)=[] -> [].
#     (Excluded by the !isFunction guard in isArrayLike.) NOT codec-faithful.
#   * Symbol primitives -> truthy, not array-like, no iterator, values(sym)=[] -> [].
#     NOT codec-faithful.
#   * boxed wrappers: new String('hi') -> array-like + isString -> stringToArray ->
#     ['h','i']; new Number(0) -> falsy? NO (object is truthy) -> not array-like ->
#     values -> []. Boxed primitives are NOT codec-faithful (identity lost).
#   * custom NON-array-like iterables (object with only Symbol.iterator) -> node 3
#     iteratorToArray. NOT codec-faithful (Symbol-keyed prop).
#   * NaN/±Infinity as the top-level VALUE: NaN is falsy -> []; ±Infinity is truthy,
#     length undefined, no iterator, values -> []. These ARE behaviorally specified
#     here but NaN/Infinity do not round-trip the codec as standalone args, so the
#     generator does not emit them as the top-level value (their result, [], is
#     trivially documented).

# =============================================================================
